 ### PyGS Optimization
-The optimization process using a combined MSE and SSIM loss, along with density control strategies adapted from 3DGS, while preserving the multi-scale structure [S13, unofficial].
-*   **Step**: Apply splitting and cloning strategies from 3DGS for density control. Newly split/cloned Gaussians remain in their original level and cluster. Routinely reassess and reassign the levels of Gaussians based on their scales to preserve the multi-scale characteristics and balanced distribution across levels [S13, unofficial].
+The optimization process using a combined MSE and SSIM loss, along with density control strategies adapted from 3DGS, while preserving the multi-scale structure [S13, unofficial]. It sets itself apart by routinely reassessing and reassigning the levels of Gaussians based on their scales to preserve multi-scale characteristics in large-scale scenes [S13, unofficial].
 
 ### GAINS (Gaussian-based Inverse Rendering from Sparse multi-view captures)
-A two-stage inverse rendering framework that leverages foundation model priors to stabilize geometry and material estimation from sparse multi-view RGB captures [S14]. It sets itself apart by using 2DGS instead of 3DGS because 2DGS provides more accurate geometry reconstruction, which is essential for accurate material property estimation [S14].
-*   **Step**:
-    1.  Stage I: Optimize geometry using monocular depth, normal, and diffusion priors [S14].
-    2.  Stage II: Jointly optimize material parameters and lighting using segmentation, intrinsic image decomposition (IID), and diffusion priors to minimize re-rendering loss [S14].
-*   **Parameter**: $N$ (Number of input views) typically ranges from 4 to 32 [S14].
+A two-stage inverse rendering framework that leverages foundation model priors to stabilize geometry and material estimation from sparse multi-view RGB captures [S14]. It sets itself apart by using 2DGS for more accurate geometry reconstruction, which is essential for accurate material property estimation, and integrating monocular depth, normal, and diffusion priors [S14].
+
+### SAGS (Adaptive Gaussian Splatting)
+A method that utilizes an adaptive 2D low-pass filter at inferencing time based on rendering resolution and camera distance to handle aliasing [S15, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from standard 3DGS by dynamically adjusting the filtering based on scene geometry and view parameters to produce aliasing-free images [S15, unofficial].
+
+### Frequency Gaussian Splatting
+A method that applies frequency-domain regularization to the rendered 2D image to encourage recovery of high-frequency detail, resolving blurring issues [S15, unofficial]. It sets itself apart by operating in the frequency domain to specifically target high-frequency loss common in standard 3DGS [S15, unofficial].
 
 ### Neural Illumination Enhancement
-A neural module applied only to non-Lambertian regions to model view-dependent highlights and handle non-Lambertian effects that vanilla 3DGS struggles with [S16, unofficial]. It sets itself apart by enabling modeling of view-dependent highlights alongside global illumination caching, unlike existing methods that struggle with non-Lambertian effects or cache only partial global illumination [S16, unofficial].
-*   **Step**:
-    1.  Input spatial position, reflection direction, and material-related features (view direction, surface normals, Fresnel term, roughness) into the MLP [S16, unofficial].
-    2.  Encode spatial positions using HashGrid encoding to capture local spatial structures [S16, unofficial].
-    3.  Encode reflection directions using Spherical Harmonics (SH) to model directional effects [S16, unofficial].
-    4.  Use other features (view direction, surface normals, Fresnel term, roughness) directly as identity features [S16, unofficial].
-    5.  Compute the final rendered image by adding the neural residual $I_n$ to the explicit Gaussian image $I_g$ [S16, unofficial].
+A neural module applied only to non-Lambertian regions to model view-dependent highlights and handle non-Lambertian effects that vanilla 3DGS struggles with [S16, unofficial]. It sets itself apart by predicting a view-dependent color residual using a lightweight MLP (with HashGrid and SH encodings) and adding it to the explicit Gaussian image, enabling modeling of view-dependent highlights alongside global illumination caching [S16, unofficial].
 
 ### GraphiGS
-A variant of the GraphiXS framework where the component distribution $\delta_\theta$ is modeled as a 3D Gaussian primitive, providing compact spatial support and exponential density decay [S17]. It sets itself apart from heavy-tailed distributions by using Gaussian primitives which provide compact spatial support but may be limited in capturing broader uncertainty regions [S17].
-*   **Formula**:
-    $$\delta_{\text{GS}}(x; \mu, \Sigma) = \exp(-1/2 (x - \mu)^T \Sigma^{-1} (x - \mu))$$
-    where $\mu$ is the mean vector (dimension $\mathbb{R}^3$) and $\Sigma$ is the covariance matrix (dimension $\mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3}$) [S17].
+A variant of the GraphiXS framework where the component distribution $\delta_\theta$ is modeled as a 3D Gaussian primitive, providing compact spatial support and exponential density decay [S17]. It sets itself apart by using Gaussian primitives to model uncertainty, offering compact spatial support compared to heavy-tailed distributions [S17].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 
-*   **Time Complexity**: The forward pass is $O(N \cdot P)$ where $N$ is the number of Gaussians and $P$ is the number of pixels, dominated by sorting and alpha blending. The backward pass is similar. Densification adds $O(N)$ overhead every 1000 iterations [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(N \cdot D)$ where $D$ is the dimensionality of Gaussian attributes (position, scale, rotation, opacity, SH coefficients). Standard 3DGS scenes can exceed 1GB for real-world datasets [12943-data-compression-and-storage-optimization].
-*   **Measured Performance**: On a Jetson AGX Orin, LS-Gaussian achieves 5.41× speedup over the edge GPU baseline and up to 17.3× speedup with a customized accelerator, rendering one frame in every 6 frames fully [S8].
-*   **Strengths**: Real-time rendering speeds, high visual fidelity, explicit representation allows for easy editing and compression [12943-data-compression-and-storage-optimization].
-*   **Limitations**: Sensitive to initialization (camera bias), struggles with sparse views and noise without pre-processing, and requires significant memory for large scenes [S4, S6, 12943-data-compression-and-storage-optimization].
-*   **When to use**: When real-time novel view synthesis is required and sufficient multi-view imagery is available. Use Corrected Scene-Extent for clustered camera setups [S4]. Use Denoise-GS for sparse/noisy inputs [S6].
+### Time and Memory Complexity
+- **Memory:** Standard 3DGS requires significant memory for millions of Gaussians, often exceeding 1GB for real-world datasets [12943-data-compression-and-storage-optimization]. Half-precision implementations reduce register pressure and memory bandwidth usage [S2].
+- **Time:** Rendering is $O(N \cdot M)$ where $N$ is pixels and $M$ is Gaussians per pixel, but optimized via tile-based sorting and rasterization [6233-optimization-and-parallelization]. Sparse rendering techniques (e.g., LS-Gaussian) reduce computation by rendering only every $k$-th frame [S8].
+
+### Measured Performance
+- **DiffGS:** 50K Gaussians: PSNR 28.61, SSIM 0.9787, LPIPS 0.0251; 100K Gaussians: PSNR 30.35, SSIM 0.9838, LPIPS 0.0151; 350K Gaussians: PSNR 34.01, SSIM 0.9879, LPIPS 0.0149 [S10].
+- **LS-Gaussian:** 5.41× speedup over edge GPU baseline on Jetson AGX Orin, up to 17.3× with custom accelerator [S8].
+
+### Strengths and Limitations
+- **Strengths:** Real-time rendering, high fidelity, differentiable optimization [6233-optimization-and-parallelization].
+- **Limitations:** Sensitive to initialization, prone to floaters, high memory usage [12943-data-compression-and-storage-optimization]. Half-precision requires careful loss scaling [S2].
+
+### When to Use
+- Use standard implementation for general high-quality novel view synthesis.
+- Use half-precision for memory-constrained GPUs or large scenes [S2].
+- Use Corrected Scene-Extent for scenes with clustered cameras [S4].
+- Use Selective Defocus Blurring for scenes with varying depth of field [S5].
+- Use Denoise-GS for sparse, noisy inputs [S6].
+- Use Multi-Scale Constraint for large-scale outdoor scenes [S7].
+- Use LS-Gaussian for real-time video stream processing [S8].
+- Use DiffGS for compact representations with fewer Gaussians [S10].
+- Use PyGS for large-scale multi-scale scenes [S13, unofficial].
+- Use GAINS for inverse rendering from sparse views [S14].
+- Use SAGS/Frequency GS for aliasing/high-frequency detail [S15, unofficial].
+- Use NIE for non-Lambertian materials [S16, unofficial].
+- Use GraphiGS for uncertainty modeling [S17].
 
 ## Background
 
-3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 as a method for real-time explicit scene representation [9128-3d-gaussian-splatting]. The implementation details described here, such as the specific densification thresholds and pruning criteria, are derived from the original paper and subsequent refinements to address stability and quality issues [S1, S4]. The use of CUDA kernels and half-precision arithmetic has been optimized in libraries like `gsplat` and `tiny-cuda-nn` to accelerate training and rendering [S2, S3]. The method has since been extended to handle defocus blur [S5], sparse views [S6], large-scale scenes [S7], and inverse rendering [S14].
+### Origin
+3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [9128-3d-gaussian-splatting]. The implementation details described here stem from subsequent research and engineering efforts to optimize, stabilize, and extend the base method for various applications.
+
+### History and Development
+- **2023:** Base 3DGS released [9128-3d-gaussian-splatting].
+- **2024-2026:** Various extensions for half-precision [S2], defocus blur [S5], denoising [S6], large-scale scenes [S7][S13, unofficial], inverse rendering [S14], and aliasing [S15, unofficial] were developed.
+
+### Usage
+Used in computer graphics, robotics (SLAM), and virtual reality for real-time novel view synthesis and scene reconstruction.
 
